      body: 'Here is a made-up story to hold the idea. A stonemason took on a young apprentice named Tomas. On the first morning the mason pointed to a great block of bedrock already set deep in the ground and said that this was what the whole house stood on. Then he handed Tomas a chisel and a small stone and told him that this was his own work. Tomas carried his small stone up the hill, set it where he was told, and he was proud — rightly proud, for it was good work. But that evening he told the village that the house stood on his stone. The mason laughed, not unkindly, and walked him back up the hill in the dark with a lamp. He put Tomas’s hand on the small stone, then made him kneel and put his hand on the bedrock underneath, cold and enormous and going down further than a man could dig. His own stone was IN the house, the mason told him; this one WAS the house. Tomas never stopped being proud of his stone. He only stopped confusing it with the ground. A small stone named after a man, resting on bedrock no man quarried — that is the picture the sentence paints: "And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it." (Matthew 16:18)',
